    </section>

    <section class="future" id="future-of-se" aria-labelledby="future-title">
      <div class="future-panel">
        <p class="eyebrow">A shared question, many perspectives</p>
        <h2 id="future-title">The Future of Software Engineering</h2>
        <div class="future-copy">
          <p>We don’t expect the future to be about producing more lines of code. AI is already speeding up routine implementation, setup, and testing—but faster output does not guarantee secure, dependable software. As those tasks become easier to automate, the harder work comes into sharper focus: deciding what is worth building, designing systems that hold together, and judging whether a proposed solution is actually correct.</p>
          <p>Our team sees engineers becoming more creative system designers, careful reviewers, and responsible guides for powerful tools. The opportunity is broader access to building and more time for meaningful problems; the challenge is to keep critical thinking, security, and human responsibility in the loop. We can’t know exactly what five or ten years will bring, but we can choose to learn the fundamentals and use AI thoughtfully along the way.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="principles" aria-label="Shared themes">
          <div class="principle"><h3>Design beyond the code</h3><p>Start with the problem, then shape the system and specify what success means.</p></div>
          <div class="principle"><h3>Verify, don’t just generate</h3><p>Review AI-assisted work for correctness, security, and reliability.</p></div>
          <div class="principle"><h3>Keep people in the loop</h3><p>Make room for curiosity, creativity, and responsible engineering judgment.</p></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>

  <footer>
