  <header class="site-header">
    <div class="wrap header-inner">
      <div class="topbar">
        <span class="wordmark">People · Ideas · Engineering</span>
        <button class="theme-toggle" id="theme-toggle" type="button" aria-label="Switch to night mode" title="Switch to night mode">☾</button>
      </div>
      <div class="hero">
        <p class="eyebrow">A team introduction</p>
        <h1>People who build what’s next.</h1>
        <p class="hero-copy">We’re computer scientists with different paths and a shared curiosity about how thoughtful engineering can make technology more useful, safe, and human.</p>
        <nav class="nav-links" aria-label="Main navigation">
          <a href="#team">Meet the team <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a>
          <a href="#future-of-se">Our view of the future <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <main class="wrap">
    <section id="team" aria-labelledby="team-title">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <div><p class="eyebrow">Four perspectives, one shared curiosity</p><h2 id="team-title">Meet the team</h2></div>
        <p class="section-intro">From data science and product-building to full-stack development, fitness, books, and gaming—here are the people behind the ideas.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="member-grid">
        <article class="member-card">
          <img src="{{image:akshay.jpg}}" alt="Photo of Akshay" width="128" height="128">
          <h3>Akshay</h3>
          <p class="study">MSc Computer Science</p>
          <p class="bio">Akshay Pradeep Kumar studies computer science and enjoys running and travelling. He has recently started to love cooking. He sees AI as a way to help developers complete tasks faster, with the potential to help people reach new heights in technology. His view is that the next era of software engineering will reward creativity in what people make, rather than coding alone.</p>
          <p class="fun-fact"><em>Fun fact: He has recently started to love cooking.</em></p>
          <p class="outlook"><strong>Looking ahead:</strong> Akshay expects software engineering to become more about creative ideas and the software people imagine than simply writing code.</p>
          <p class="hot-take">🔥 We’re teaching machines to code so we can finally spend our time arguing about what to build.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="member-card">
          <img src="{{image:leman.png}}" alt="Photo of Leman" width="128" height="128">
          <h3>Leman</h3>
          <p class="study">M.Sc. 5th semester, Universität Paderborn; B.Sc., Ahsanullah University of Science and Technology, Bangladesh</p>
          <p class="bio">Leman is completing an M.Sc. at Universität Paderborn after earning a B.Sc. from Ahsanullah University of Science and Technology in Bangladesh. Leman is interested in data science, video gaming, and reading books. Leman sees AI as a mixed influence on software engineering: it quickly handles menial setup and repetitive tasks, while also affecting the job sector. In five to ten years, Leman expects engineers to design whole systems with AI rather than write code line by line.</p>
          <p class="fun-fact"><em>Fun fact: People say Leman is a bit introverted, but Leman doesn’t agree.</em></p>
          <p class="outlook"><strong>Looking ahead:</strong> Leman expects boilerplate and even workflows from app building to shipment to become increasingly automated, making system design and effective use of AI tools more important.</p>
          <p class="hot-take">🔥 AI won’t replace good software developers. Better developers are who that can use AI tools better.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="member-card">
          <img src="{{image:mazhar.png}}" alt="Photo of Mazhar" width="128" height="128">
          <h3>Mazhar</h3>
          <p class="study">First-semester M.Sc. Computer Science, Paderborn University; B.Sc. CSE, Leading University, Bangladesh</p>
          <p class="bio">Mazhar is a first-semester M.Sc. Computer Science student at Paderborn University, with a B.Sc. in Computer Science and Engineering from Leading University in Bangladesh and three and a half years of industry experience. He enjoys building products, automating repetitive tasks, learning architecture and security, and exchanging ideas. He sees AI reducing routine implementation work, putting more weight on fundamentals such as reliability and security. He also recognizes that AI can make overlooked weaknesses easier to exploit.</p>
          <p class="fun-fact"><em>Fun fact: After three and a half years in software, he’s back at university learning to delegate to AI and hoping it still leaves him a job.</em></p>
          <p class="outlook"><strong>Looking ahead:</strong> Mazhar finds the pace of progress daunting and worries that AI could advance faster than safe practices and everyday adaptation. He hopes guidance, security, and responsible use keep pace with capability, especially as automation changes people’s roles.</p>
          <p class="portfolio-link"><a href="https://cloud-007.github.io/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Explore Mazhar’s portfolio ↗</a></p>
          <p class="hot-take">🔥 AI puts building within more people’s reach; curiosity, creativity and learning how to guide it make the difference.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="member-card">
          <img src="{{image:sachin.jpg}}" alt="Photo of Sachin" width="128" height="128">
          <h3>Sachin</h3>
          <p class="study">M.Sc. Computer Science, 1st semester, University of Paderborn; B.E. CSE, DSCE, Bangalore, India</p>
          <p class="bio">Sachin is a first-semester M.Sc. Computer Science student at the University of Paderborn, after a B.E. in Computer Science and Engineering from DSCE in Bangalore. He enjoys exploring AI and full-stack development, fitness, cooking, and competitive gaming. He finds AI useful for routine setup and standard test scripts, but warns that blindly trusting generated code can hide logic vulnerabilities. For him, AI amplifies capable engineers but can become a trap for beginners.</p>
          <p class="fun-fact"><em>Fun fact: He once spent three hours debugging a production error before finding his own first name misspelled in a configuration string.</em></p>
          <p class="outlook"><strong>Looking ahead:</strong> Sachin expects raw, manually written code to become a legacy workflow. He sees future engineers acting as architects and judges, writing strict specifications, designing prompt boundaries, and validating results continuously.</p>
          <p class="hot-take">🔥 AI shouldn’t replace our critical thinking; it should augment it. Outsourcing our logic to algorithms breeds intellectual laziness.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="future" id="future-of-se" aria-labelledby="future-title">
      <div class="future-panel">
        <p class="eyebrow">A shared question, many perspectives</p>
        <h2 id="future-title">The Future of Software Engineering</h2>
        <div class="future-copy">
          <p>We don’t expect the future to be about producing more lines of code. AI is already speeding up routine implementation, setup, and testing—but faster output does not guarantee secure, dependable software. As those tasks become easier to automate, the harder work comes into sharper focus: deciding what is worth building, designing systems that hold together, and judging whether a proposed solution is actually correct.</p>
          <p>Our team sees engineers becoming more creative system designers, careful reviewers, and responsible guides for powerful tools. The opportunity is broader access to building and more time for meaningful problems; the challenge is to keep critical thinking, security, and human responsibility in the loop. We can’t know exactly what five or ten years will bring, but we can choose to learn the fundamentals and use AI thoughtfully along the way.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="principles" aria-label="Shared themes">
          <div class="principle"><h3>Design beyond the code</h3><p>Start with the problem, then shape the system and specify what success means.</p></div>
          <div class="principle"><h3>Verify, don’t just generate</h3><p>Review AI-assisted work for correctness, security, and reliability.</p></div>
          <div class="principle"><h3>Keep people in the loop</h3><p>Make room for curiosity, creativity, and responsible engineering judgment.</p></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>

  <footer>
    <div class="wrap">
      <p>Built with curiosity and caffeine.</p>
      <p><a href="https://www.uni-paderborn.de/en/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">University of Paderborn ↗</a></p>
    </div>
  </footer>
